<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Vaiga</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1224 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ancient Estonian district on the western shore of Lake Peipus, contested between Novgorod and the Livonian crusaders.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Vaiga, called Waiga in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> sources, lay north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a> between the Emajõgi river and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-peipus">Lake Peipus</a>. It is known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-livonia">Henry of Livonia's</a> chronicle as one of the smaller Estonian lands of the early 13th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its people farmed and fished on the lake shore and were exposed to Rus' influence; Novgorod and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> raided and levied tribute in the area. Some inhabitants accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> baptism before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> missionaries arrived. Its elders are not named in the sources.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Vaiga was conquered by the crusaders in the campaigns of 1220-1224 and became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bishopric-of-dorpat">Bishopric of Dorpat</a>, on the Livonian border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> and Novgorod.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/vaiga.jpg" alt="Vaiga" width="320" height="219"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. 藏骨集团 assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Estonian_counties.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1224 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Estonian paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Estonian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/estonia">Estonia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Waiga</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1012&amp;focus=vaiga" aria-label="Vaiga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5252 201 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Vaiga lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5572 457L5568 464"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5568 464h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5572 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5572 457h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1012&amp;focus=vaiga">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
